# Season Three — After the Broken Ball

The Bronze Ball has left Atomer's ballroom broken open and its people vulnerable to the darkness below. The Darkest Delvers have survived, but companions and guests are missing, Cassius is under investigation, and the guild has forbidden its troublesome heroes to adventure. Saving their city will require more than another commission.[^horizon]

## 1. The broken night

While the guild struggles with the catastrophe, Evelyn finds the Gentleman Cavalier Traveler preparing to leave. He speaks of companions cursed beneath Minotaur Mesa and an evil heart that may still bind them. She promises to seek it. The red portal closes before their reunion can become the life she has imagined.

Koh takes Anathala home, but the next morning brings the reality of suspension: no ordinary guild work or pay. The companions volunteer to clear monsters from the damaged bank. Among its deposit boxes Koh takes wealthy clients' money and Withers's gems. Krandal objects. Elsewhere in the building they kill a hook horror and find its egg. The cleric insists on keeping it, believing the creature might be raised differently from its parent.

Their relief work does not exempt them from the grounding. Guild officers stop the Delvers after further cleanup, and Vestal takes Evelyn back to school. Beneath its fountain the teacher reveals another heritage: Loran's institution once trained heroic young women. The heart-shaped handle becomes a blade when Evelyn remembers her own good deeds, but she cannot yet open the sanctuary's inner door. She leaves to face the family who still mistake her strength for frailty.

At the farm, the serpent warrior Nopa Ropa swears service until Krandal rescues Princess Hissia, whom he believes taken below Atomer. He begins digging irrigation channels. Goodwine and his son offer an enormous potato-growing investment. Krandal accepts a book and plow, while withholding the ownership and profit commitments they seek.

Koh brings his stolen gems to the Cloven Brotherhood for fencing, bargaining for distance from his family without freeing himself from its attention. When guild officials question the family, he makes a more lasting disclosure: Penelope learns that her father is One Shot, the adventurer who helped defeat the dragon whose scale he gave her.

The guild can still use his gifts. Permitted to help the afflicted, Koh enters seven elves' mental prisons with Krandal, whose curse-removing prayers free them. Each recovery matters, but many victims remain. Evelyn travels toward her parents, Krandal returns to the obligations gathering around his farm, and Koh begins the work of helping others find the homecoming he fought to give his wife.[^broken-night]

## 2. The lives they choose

Evelyn's journey ends in industrial Vermicity, where her parents intend to marry her to Julian Thomas. She refuses both the proposal and their insistence that her adventuring must be illness. Supervision tightens until a vampire enters her bedroom. In her nightclothes, Evelyn resists its charm and fights with hammer and radiant magic. Her parents see her drive it into the street. A vampire hunter finishes the creature, but the Blackwoods finally recognize their daughter's power and chosen life. She leaves on a summoned celestial elk, promising to visit.

In Atomer, daily treatment cannot keep pace with newly afflicted elves. Carrie uses the red dream-shard and Anathala's freely given blood to construct a detector for Koh. It glows near a disturbed elf outside his home. Koh subdues the victim without killing him; the elf later wakes lucid, suggesting that some compulsions lie nearer the surface than the deeper dream prisons. Veggie Sticks helps Carrie give the amulet a directional guide. Koh receives a stipend for this special work and moves his family to a smaller house as the financial district is enclosed.

Krandal buys the donkey Sean, takes on Muck Muck and invests in a potato-sprouting barn. A hollowed, mummified attacker strikes the farm and says someone has offered a reward for Krandal's death. The cleric kills it, but neither its bat insignia nor Carrie's examination identifies the employer. His crop improves while the larger danger persists.

Three months pass. Atomer becomes Silver City, its underground perils offered as licensed adventures to a flood of newcomers. Dumbledwark of Raisinbunch, a veteran newspaper correspondent, arrives to investigate the grounded heroes. Koh resists intrusive questions; Evelyn defends Cassius. Krandal accepts a gilded potato and voluntarily answers a truth-bound interview, revealing Koh's bank theft to the reporter without consenting to another interrogation of his friend.

Zoltar, occupying Cassius's office, offers a different account. He acknowledges the Academy's profit in maintaining the dungeon business, whereas Cassius wanted Khalazar defeated. Dumbledwark persuades him to license a C4 expedition with the Delvers. While Zoltar fetches marmalade, the reporter steals a warlock's tome from the office crates.

The licence is real; the promised end to the Delvers' grounding remains uncertain. Their next adventure may furnish the newspaper with heroes, the Academy with profitable publicity, or the dungeon with more casualties.[^changing-city]

## 3. Licensed depths

Dumbledwark accompanies the three beneath the ruined ballroom, through mirrored passages and Gruber's lift into C4. An old geared crank from Glepo Ma's collection fits the dungeon machinery. Water and effort turn its great wheel and open the way.

The rooms beyond hold remnants of earlier visitors. Boots recovered from a drain contain severed feet; Krandal takes the enchanted footwear. Koh pockets a numbered shield, Evelyn finds a blue crystal in a broken golem, and Dumbledwark questions a crushed corpse about its killer. A behir hunts the caves. Another supposed casualty, Iron Monkey, is alive, hiding in the creature's dung.

Evelyn calls the beast out. Lightning wounds her and Krandal, while Koh darts between shadows with psychic blades and the reporter supports them from cover. The behir turns upon Iron Monkey and tears away an arm. Krandal's spectral mace finally kills it. As the huge body falls toward Evelyn, Koh catches her hand and carries them clear through magic. The recovered crystal later opens their exit; the monk's survival has already undermined the certainty of the guild's casualty list.[^c4]

The next special assignment calls for diplomacy. Destroying Angel, a Silver centaur envoy, leads the Delvers to Deep Winds Spa, where flumphs might offer underground travelers shelter after the sunlight mirrors fail. Angel presents a jar of surface air and observes the hosts' customs. Their visitors include skeletal minotaurs, trolls, a roper and a towel-wrapped beholder, sharing neutral ground rather than fighting.

Koh luxuriates in the baths while his friends find the hospitality less easy. Evelyn privately questions a three-eyed mind-reader about the Traveler; Krandal encounters the same visitor's interest in his island memories. Its claims do not become knowledge shared by every companion. Angel comforts a grieving ghost before the headmistress asks for help: black sand has accumulated in the filters, and something has frightened the staff away.

In the works, daylight exposes black ooze containing a thin, long-clawed figure. It engulfs Krandal and flees toward the baths. The others pursue, but blades and arrows cannot free him until Koh thrusts his illuminated drift globe into the mass. Krandal tumbles out. Koh kills the escaping creature with a final psychic strike.

The filters are clear when inspected again. The spa has reason to welcome adventurers, though no permanent guild refuge has been built. As Krandal is led away to recover, a private door silently calls Koh's name.[^spa]

## 4. The sealed way

Koh enters the aboleth's bath and accepts its invitation to a contest of minds. Twice he overcomes it. He sees a lake throne and servants, then a skull-framed triangle gate with three recesses before it. The creature claims Khalazar needs Dolbluth blood to regenerate; another glimpse shows its own ambitions for worship and domination. Koh leaves with warnings and images, rather than proof that all its promises are true.

After a night at the spa, the Delvers discover stairs descending past three dead Silver adventurers. They reach a catacomb of circular tomb covers, black sand and the gate Koh saw. Its altar has three empty hollows. Mummies emerge as the party investigates. One paralyzes Krandal and lifts him toward a vacant niche until Koh breaks its concentration. Evelyn hunts an invisible attacker by its dusty tracks. They retreat with the door still closed.

Carrie receives the fallen adventurers' badges and identifies the recovered ring and necklace. Their visit does not lift the grounding. Evelyn later opens Loran's sanctuary with her blade's lance form, but its radiant armor remains beyond her grasp. At the farm, Krandal discovers that his saved egg has hatched. A little armored hook horror rubs affectionately against his ankle.

Koh obtains a model for a forged lift pass. With Krandal's bluff supporting the false papers, the trio returns underground without permission, the hatchling hidden among his provisions. The uncovered detector leads them past the catacomb stairs to a separate chamber of black slime, three distant platforms and creature-headed keys.

Goggles Granny, a heavily laden feline traveler, helps decipher its ancient inscription. Crossing the pool is dangerous: Evelyn falls from her boat and struggles ashore, Koh balances above it with his sash, and Granny enlarges herself to throw a diminished Krandal across on a tether. Incorrect settings answer with lightning. At last they align the levers' light and turn the keys, which disappear into the locks as the slime drains.

Below lies a bat-capped vessel containing a dried heart. Koh holds its pressure plate while Granny weighs the jar. Stone and measuring weights replace it without triggering the trap. Evelyn carries the vessel away. Granny turns back safely before the long descent, leaving the three to place their prize in one hollow before the triangle gate. Two remain empty.[^first-vessel]

## 5. The lives that never happened

Beyond the left stair the Delvers lose their way, and each seems to return alone to Atomer. Krandal faces unpaid workers in revolt and Goodwines who claim to own his farm. Koh finds wanted posters and an Anathala who appears to reject him beside Zoltar. Evelyn's sanctuary becomes an asylum whose keepers insist she has never been a paladin.

These lives are constructed from fear. Krandal glimpses rats beneath his accusers' shapes; Koh attacks the false Zoltar without killing the living man. Evelyn raises her unusual mirror and sees a rat fastened to the back of her head. Refusing the imposed story, she wakes where their bodies truly lie.

Koh and Krandal meet an armored knight inside the dissolving nightmare. He calls himself Radiant Lance and says his mind has been trapped for generations in the Black Basilica. He cannot release them, but offers Krandal companionship and a final game of cards while Evelyn acts outside.

In the physical hall, chains of rats connect the sleeping companions and an armored skeleton to a floating congregation. Evelyn cuts the rats from her friends' necks. The All Rat speaks with one voice and offers a hook-horror-headed urn while asking to keep its contents, which it claims are Khalazar's and the source of its strength. The Delvers refuse an empty vessel.

The fight alternates real blows with disorienting images of older adventures. Krandal heals, Evelyn attacks and Koh finds another answer: he leaps into the swarm, tears the entire urn free and teleports away with it. The congregation's light fades. Its chains unravel into frightened, scattering rats.

They examine the knight's physical remains. Krandal takes the surviving Pelorian tabard and the party stores his lance. The little hook horror, now called Scroungy, receives a piece of Evelyn's pink ribbon. When adult hook horrors approach, Koh kills the one threatening Krandal; the others retreat and the youngling stays with him.

They retrieve the first vessel from the triangle altar rather than leave it behind. Evelyn now carries two urns, while all three hollows stand empty. Before risking the remaining path they return to the spa and pay its steep new price for rooms. The farm was never sold, no public bounty was posted, and Evelyn was never imprisoned in an asylum; the damage they escaped was a prison of the mind.[^all-rat]

## 6. The third vessel

Rested after their expensive night, the companions take the right-hand route from the triangle gate. Bones and carapaces crunch beneath their feet. What seems a threatening guardian's voice introduces a much friendlier stranger: Barclay, a tortle cleric of Ptah who says he has been stranded underground for months. His lantern companion Chance offers a spectral hand in greeting. Glad of company, Barclay joins them.

Four safe pressure plates open two great doors. Koh and Evelyn take one corridor, Krandal and Barclay the other, coordinating through silent speech. Bridges on one side depend on switches across the divide. Wall inscriptions explain the opposite floor's symbols. A mistaken setting threatens gas before they correct it, but four more plates release a pair of stone golems.

Their attacks produce baffling consequences: damage to one construct strikes the other. Koh's psychic blades cannot wound mindless stone, and he changes to steel while the clerics heal and strengthen their companions. Evelyn disperses poisonous air and sends lightning across the linked chambers. Krandal's spectral mace brings the damaged golems down together.

Beyond stands a huge tower whose vents make the chamber breathe. Gricks wait among rocks and debris. Koh moves into concealment while Evelyn learns to ascend between the jets, matching her steps to their rhythm. At its summit she finds a grick-capped urn. Removing it stills the wind and wakes the predators. Koh strikes from hiding, killing the enormous alpha as the others contend with smaller creatures. Evelyn secures the third vessel beside the first two.

They choose rest and preparation before opening the gate. Their return follows the old forest entrance, now fitted with bridges over its traps. Veggie Sticks dislikes another disturbance, but agrees to help them reach Atomer through the trees. First Barclay hides the trio from divination. Their Silver badges promptly fall. The companions suspect tracking, although the effect alone does not tell them exactly what the medals do.

A cedar near a town well becomes their way home. They emerge among startled citizens, carrying all three urns while the sealed door remains below. Chance's identity, the contents of the unopened vessels and the guild's reason for keeping them out of the depths remain questions for another encounter. They have found the means to continue; they still need the freedom to use it.[^third-vessel]

## 7. Atomer's reckoning

Evelyn takes charge of the badges and passes the three urns to Koh. She calls her companions to an evening confrontation at the guild, then rides to Loran's school. Koh returns to a barricaded home. His missed night of reassuring telepathy has left Anathala terrified; he comforts her and learns of their daughter's desert dreams. A letter from Dumbledwark gives him a concrete lead: Cassius is held at Wildfire's estate.

Boonefellow intercepts Krandal but lets him pass, asking him to look for their missing friend Phelan. At the farm the Goodwines have bought potatoes, rather than the land of the rat-induced nightmare. Krandal pays his workers from the proceeds. Nopa Ropa throws the money aside: his concern is Hissia. Krandal recalls the aboleth's claim that she labors on Khalazar's machine in the deepest Dawnless Dunes. Nopa swears to join the rescue.

At school, Evelyn again fails to reach the armor beyond Loran's ward. Then a warning bell and screams summon her attention above. A hook horror has broken into the school. Protecting the girls matters more than claiming a private prize; this time she reaches the radiant plates, dons them and destroys the intruder.

She brings a crowd to the guild. Zoltar admits that divination tracks its adventurers, then defends Silver City's prosperity. The Delvers ask about the people it has cost. Clearstone produces thirty names of missing elves, and the crowd hears familiar neighbors called aloud. Guild members cross to the heroes' side. Koh presses Zoltar to bring Cassius back, following him with the townspeople to the Broscale estate.

Valentine emerges with the living guildmaster. Zoltar rides away under warning. Restored to his desk, Cassius authorizes the expedition and supplies each Delver with funds. He gives Koh a ring of free action and asks that it return with him. They buy protection and healing supplies; Koh passes his falling-protection ring to Krandal in exchange for a potato.

Another spa recollection gives Krandal a harder duty. The aboleth said that defeating Khalazar would end the skeletal minotaurs' curse and their animation. He tells Chad, then seeks the warriors' own wishes beneath Noble Hollow. Corgan Greathorns and his companions desire final rest, but will fight to win it. Krandal reassembles Chad's skeletal body, and ten minotaurs pledge their aid alongside Nopa.

After their night's rest, Pelor grants Krandal a potato-shaped censer of radiant and healing power. Cassius is free, the allies have chosen their purpose, and the three vessels are ready for the descent.[^reckoning]

## 8. Beyond the triangle gate

The authorized expedition descends with trusted guild companions, Nopa Ropa and the ten skeletal minotaurs. Others guard the upper approaches. Darmah Edstream brings the chalk and magic for two evacuation circles. Evelyn travels with her elk Albert; Koh carries the three urns and the detector made with his wife's blood.

At the altar, each Delver inserts a vessel. Their golden heads turn toward the gate. A mummy emerges and speaks into the three friends' minds, unheard by their startled allies. She calls herself Shaolin Nym, architect of the Dolbluth, and describes the emperor they abandoned after his betrayal. Earlier challengers destroyed his machines, she says, without ending his returns. They must restore the organs to his body and defeat him in his true form. She also points to the ancestral blood at Koh's wrist.

The vessels lift from their sockets and the gate opens. Nym crumbles as the company enters the windless desert beyond. The door closes behind them. Their passage leads past buried buildings and a shattered blue crystal that resembles the light of Khalazar's mental city. Koh gathers a fragment; his new ring carries him across deep sand where the others struggle. They avoid a hidden roper and approach the black pyramid.

Gaunt guards watch enslaved workers among obelisks and green flame. Koh and Dominic remove the sentries, then use nonlethal strikes to render civilians unconscious for transport. A noisy slide down the dune brings a command from above, and the remaining workers begin filing into the pyramid.

Darmah gives Koh the power to climb its wall. In the upper chamber he sees a withered, jeweled body attended by Keeves and a woman who resembles Hissia. Below, Dominic finds an unfinished statue fed by black liquid. Wildfire and Withers labor there with the other captives. They have survived their fall, but remain under control.

Koh's ring thwarts a guardian's magical restraint as he searches further. Then an enormous skeletal bat arrives at the balcony, clicking as it scans the room. Concealment may no longer protect him. He retreats toward the workshop, where the others must gather people faster than the danger can find them.

They consider waking captives by removing curses, but every spell would consume strength needed against Khalazar. Instead Evelyn rides Albert out across the dunes, lance ready, drawing the bat after her. The guild parties and minotaurs hurry to the prisoners while Darmah begins marking the stone floor. The way home is being prepared; no one has yet passed through.[^gate-entry]

## 9. The heart beneath the dunes

Evelyn confronts the bat alone. Radiance tears its shadowed wings, but its scream banishes Albert and leaves her on the sand. She stands again, changes lance to sword and breaks the skeleton apart. Albert can be called back; the shattered bat lies still.[^bat]

Inside the pyramid, the rescuers abandon the proposed curse-removal treatment and carry stable, unconscious captives to Darmah. Her first circle opens. Eight minotaurs take people through to the guild, including Wildfire and Withers among those evacuated. Boonefellow carries Phelan home. Chad and Corgan stay for the remaining work.

Evelyn returns and summons Albert again before they go upstairs. Passing the unfinished statue, she cuts its black-fluid tubes without bringing down the entire structure. Koh finds more starving workers in two provision rooms and subdues them for rescue. The bedchamber attendants are now identified: Hissia and Keeves. Koh and Dominic knock them unconscious, and Nopa takes the princess in his arms. They are carried toward the second evacuation route while the Delvers approach the still body.[^rescue]

Koh produces the three urns. Evelyn turns Khalazar over, exposing the emptied cavities behind skull and chest. Together the friends replace the ancient organs. The dry heart begins to beat.

A shared vision carries them through the emperor's life: a luminous city, a growing tyrant, destroyed shrines and a conspiracy that removes brain, lungs and heart. Earlier heroes break his machines without destroying the host. Then the city seems restored around them. Khalazar strides out to face them, and Koh's mental bond draws Dominic and Darmah into the confrontation; Evelyn's bond brings Albert too.[^rite]

The city is a battlefield of locusts, light and shifting sand. Koh's psychic strike breaks Khalazar's protection. Evelyn closes with radiant blows while Krandal heals and Darmah throws fire. The emperor paralyzes and terrifies his enemies, finding them wherever they seek safety. Evelyn falls. Krandal is struck down and comes perilously close to death before Koh reaches him with a supreme healing potion. Revived, the cleric heals Evelyn in turn. Dominic wounds the emperor and is stunned; Darmah herself falls under his assault.

Koh leaps onto a roof and strikes with the rapier named for Anathala. The restored city vanishes. Back in the real bedchamber, all three Delvers press the spectral dagger into Khalazar's heart. It beats, bursts, and his body disintegrates. This time the enemy is destroyed.[^victory]

The pyramid collapses. Its falling stone takes the unfinished statue with it. Darmah calls them to the second circle, and Chad and Corgan shelter the escaping companions with their weapons. They reach the guild basement before the ruin closes over them.

Healers tend the rescued people. Hissia wakes and kisses Nopa, recognizing the love they had called forbidden. The ten minotaurs' curse ends. Chad and Corgan give Krandal a last acknowledgment as their bones become still. They have won the rest they chose; the three Delvers have all returned alive.[^home]

## One year later

The underground creatures grow fewer, and Noble Hollow's damaged earth becomes fertile. Scroungy matures into a tame beast of burden beside the farm donkey. Krandal pays his debts, hires workers, grows celebrated potatoes and offers healing where he can. Koh returns to Anathala and Penelope, hangs Anathala's Will above the hearth and teaches younger guild adventurers. Evelyn protects Atomer, helps the school rebuild and finds room for both her family duties and the life she chose. She still searches and waits for the Cavalier.

The rebuilt farm tower becomes the Delvers' home. A faded pink ribbon, a family locket and a half-eaten potato share its table with two letters. One invites guests to the wedding of Evelyn Blackwood and Lord Ethan Travail, signed GCT. The other is a red letter from the kingdom and remains unopened. The invitation offers a future beyond these adventures; the letter keeps its secrets.[^epilogue]

[^horizon]: Delvers-only synthesis through [S3E12](../episodes/S3E12.md), the owner-confirmed third-arc and entire played-campaign finale. This consolidation reviews the old narrative and existing episode evidence maps/continuity cautions; it performs no new ASR interpretation, listening, map inspection or character-sheet review. Source identities and proper names retain the limits in the [review queue](../REVIEW_QUEUE.md). [Pre-edit reading copy](../../research/legacy/2026-10-26/delvers-season-three-consolidation/delvers/narrative/SEASON_THREE.md) · [Coverage report](../../reports/DELVERS_SEASON_THREE_CONSOLIDATION_2026-10-26.md). Earlier dossier cutoffs remain historical states, not rewritten by later outcomes.
[^broken-night]: [S3E01](../episodes/S3E01.md), immediate aftermath through seven dream rescues. Same night/day follows the Bronze Ball; the three-month transition is not complete here. Kyle selects **Anathala** in the opening, with orthography still audio/written-source dependent; this remains the earlier Anthela, not a new wife. Koh actually steals/fences bank loot, but 40% terms and estimated gross value do not establish a settled cash payout. Krandal objects. The hook-horror egg is called Samuel and remains unhatched here. Loran's blade manifests but the inner door does not open. Nopa's Hissia account is testimony; Goodwine's investment is not accepted ownership. Seven recoveries do not certify all victims cured. Exact custody interviews, incomplete missing-schoolgirl counts and detailed transactions remain in the dossier/archive.
[^changing-city]: [S3E02](../episodes/S3E02.md) and [S3E03](../episodes/S3E03.md). Evelyn refuses Julian, fights a real vampire and receives parental recognition; the vampire's final kill is another hunter's. Sean, Muck Muck and the paid barn are actual acquisitions; sale to Goodwine is not performed. The assassin's patron remains unknown. Anathala willingly supplies blood for Carrie's detector; later direction upgrade occurs, but no guaranteed range or universal disease test is inferred. The shallow-compulsion recovery differs from deep dream treatment and from unhealed vocal-cord damage. S3E02 completes three months and establishes Koh's new house/special stipend. S3E03's reporter learns bank theft from Krandal's voluntary Zone of Truth, not a public confession by Koh; no consent to expose every family secret is assumed. Zoltar's economic motive is his admission, other accusations remain claims. C4 licence is obtained, tome stolen, general ungrounding merely promised. Detailed interviews, receipts, prophecy provenance and gilded-potato/compost exchanges remain in the preserved narrative/dossier.
[^c4]: [S3E04](../episodes/S3E04.md). Specific C4 paperwork is accepted, not general mission freedom. Blue crystal is recovered from an already defeated golem and later enables the exit in GM end narration; entry initially opens by button. Original gear-crank is retrieved into Evelyn's bag. Krandal takes Boots of Speed, Koh shield 87, Evelyn a golden mace; no invented properties/owners for all remains. Iron Monkey's survival revises an alleged six-dead tally, and his lost arm receives no shown treatment. Krandal kills the behir; Koh teleports Evelyn clear. Newspaper publication, reward and Cassius disposition are not supplied by the expedition.
[^spa]: [S3E05](../episodes/S3E05.md). Destroying Angel is a guest centaur diplomat, not a permanent fourth Delver. Spa neutrality does not establish species-wide peaceful behavior. Evelyn privately hears a Traveler–Khalazar curse claim; Krandal privately hears an offer of Hissia information with an immediately qualified apparent death statement. No automatic disclosure to their companions or independent truth is assumed. Specific later Hissia/minotaur answers are presented in S3E10's retrospective spa scenes. Koh's own entered interview happens in S3E06, following this episode's invitation. Koh frees Krandal with Drift Globe Daylight and kills the ooze-covered figure; its species is not settled. Cleared filters are observed, but no completed guild camp or signed alliance is shown.
[^first-vessel]: [S3E06](../episodes/S3E06.md), entered aboleth interview, completed spa night, catacomb discovery and return; [S3E07](../episodes/S3E07.md), actual unauthorized pass/entry and puzzle. Aboleth's lake/servant visions and Dolbluth-blood claim remain attributed; worship/slaughter ambitions are not present spa events. Corpse badges go to Carrie; Ring of Evasion and Amulet of Health return to Koh with later off-air allocation unsettled. Loran lance opens sanctuary before armor is obtainable. Hatchling emerges in S3E06 and is neither trained nor a named combat pet in S3E07. Covered amulet in S3E06 is not a negative test; uncovered detector glows in S3E07. Local bat/grick/hook-horror metal keys are consumed by the slime-room puzzle, distinct from the triangle gate's urns. Actual jar replacement is **23-pound rock plus two pounds of weights**; shirt/mayonnaise is an unexecuted proposal. First vessel is installed at the old altar at S3E07 cutoff. Price/HP, translation and item details remain in the archive/dossiers.
[^all-rat]: [S3E08](../episodes/S3E08.md). Farm foreclosure/strike, wanted posters, apparent Zoltar killing, asylum, imagined divine rejection and floating-pyramid city are projections, not ordinary played town history. Radiant Lance's chronology and identity are his mental testimony, alongside separately found physical remains. The hook-horror vessel stays sealed; its organ's claimed source/power is initially the All Rat's account, with brain identification strengthened by S3E09 GM recap rather than an independent opening. Koh removes it and disperses the collective without exterminating all rats. Tabard is worn by Krandal and lance stored; additional powers are not supplied. Scroungy is the affectionate later name of the saved hatchling, with a ribbon attached, not a newly found adult. First urn is retrieved: two carried by Evelyn, three old slots empty. Spa stay is paid here and completed in S3E09.
[^third-vessel]: [S3E09](../episodes/S3E09.md). Barclay's surname remains Vandergeist/Bendergeist in sources; Chance's connection is intentionally unrevealed, not established identity evidence. Paired corridors link bridges, symbols and damage; psychic critical does not kill a mindless golem. Third vessel's grick cap is visible, contents unopened, all three now in Evelyn's bag. Forest exit and Veggie's tree transport are performed, not a guild-lift return. Nondetection causes falling badges, but tracking is a hypothesis until Zoltar's later admission. Discarded-badge recovery is unshown before S3E10 handovers. Krandal's actual spa inquiry answers are deferred until S3E10, not invented into S3E09. Grick combat specifics, surviving creatures and device mechanics remain in the source dossier.
[^reckoning]: [S3E10](../episodes/S3E10.md). Urns pass Evelyn→Koh; badge handovers do not explain earlier discards. Dumbledwark's letter is a lead subsequently corroborated by Zoltar and Cassius's physical release. Goodwines buy produce, not title; about 400 gold is received and 95 each paid to three workers, Nopa rejecting it. Hissia's alleged machine work and minotaurs' expected final death are **retrospective spa conversations actually presented here**, not fresh town testimony or proven curse mechanics before the finale. Armor is claimed during school danger. Thirty-name list differs from the later approximately 37-person expedition list; neither is a rescue tally. Cassius resumes leadership in practice, not a documented legal verdict; Zoltar leaves alive. Twenty thousand gold each funds preparations. Nine crypt warriors plus rebuilt Chad make **ten total**, not ten plus Chad; skeletal reanimation is not living resurrection. A long rest and next dawn's censer precede the expedition: no extra unplayed day is inserted from the old narrative's repeated morning phrasing.
[^gate-entry]: [S3E11](../episodes/S3E11.md). Guild allies include Brigand Patrol, First Sons and Five Emissaries; Viper Gang does not enter. Shaolin Nym/Nim, Dolbluth and Emperor Orgoth Argus/Argir are uncertain spellings; her ancient history and ritual instruction remain NPC testimony. Three jars inserted then released, gate entered and closed behind; no separately narrated rebagging scene is invented. S3E12 confirms jars are again in Koh's bag. Crystal shard is collected, not declared a key. Guards defeated and stable nonlethal workers are distinct. Workshop physically contains Withers/Wildfire; Hissia's identity is suspected before later confirmation. Statue and dismantled spider are observed but not fought. Darmah casts Spider Climb and prepares circles, without completed evacuation in S3E11. Bat pursuit begins; fight concludes next episode. Exact named roster, weapons and missing-person list stay in dossier/archive.
[^bat]: [S3E12](../episodes/S3E12.md), ~00:00–00:19. Evelyn destroys the skeletal bat with radiant lance/sword. Albert is banished, not permanently killed, and later resummoned as the same steed. No assistance is inserted into her solo kill.
[^rescue]: [S3E12](../episodes/S3E12.md), ~00:19–01:00. Proposed Remove Curse is explicitly retracted, with spell slots refunded; no such captive cures are written into the narrative. First circle is actually used; eight minotaurs carry stable people while Chad/Corgan remain. Inconsistent counts do not establish a total rescued or “37 of 37.” Two additional provision rooms hold six and eight workers, all subdued; poor food/water is inferred from condition, not proved poison. Hissia is positively identified now, carried by Nopa; Corgan carries Keeves. Tube cutting stops supply without immediately destroying the whole statue. Broken giant statue's alternate iron-golem encounter belongs to post-campaign commentary, not this played rescue.
[^rite]: [S3E12](../episodes/S3E12.md), ~01:00–01:24. Actual brain/heart/lung organs are placed in hollow cavities at the **back** of Khalazar's body. A separately applied drop of Koh's wrist blood is instructed earlier but not explicitly performed here. Shared ritual vision depicts a tyrant and removal of his organs; ancestral identities, Anthela-like resemblance and three-eyed figure are not independently verified genealogy. Apparent restored city is later exposed as illusion, not a physically rebuilt city. Dominic/Darmah participate through Koh's current network, Albert through Evelyn's bond; other guild members/minotaurs are not automatically drawn in.
[^victory]: [S3E12](../episodes/S3E12.md), ~01:24–02:40. Evelyn and Krandal fall unconscious; Krandal's natural-one death save gives two failures, not death. Koh's supreme potion restores him, then Krandal heals Evelyn. Darmah is downed yet later participates in escape; exact intervening revival is unshown. Command does not literally force undead Khalazar prone; GM grants an advantaged attack as dramatic adjudication. Rapier **Anathala's Will / Anathela's Will**, named for Koh's wife, leads into the physical three-person spectral-dagger thrust. Poison is ineffective; no poison kill. Heart bursts and Khalazar disintegrates: this is the real final defeat, unlike the second-arc shade.
[^home]: [S3E12](../episodes/S3E12.md), ~02:40–02:43 and preceding escape. Both rescue circles are used and the pyramid/unfinished statue collapse. Surviving heroes/allies return; Hissia wakes and kisses Nopa. All ten skeletal minotaurs end as inert bones, including the first eight already sent through. No full victim-by-victim rescue audit, every minor member's fate or universal monster extermination is established.
[^epilogue]: [S3E12](../episodes/S3E12.md), ~02:43–02:46:59 for epilogue/campaign ending; the older 02:49:11.950 endpoint is the later outro. One-year character outcomes and rebuilt tower are narrated as completed epilogue, not merely intended plans. Main prose shortens the groom's recorded name to **Lord Ethan Travail**; uncertain middle rendering **Renald/Renault** remains here for review. Invitation is signed GCT, but precise groom/Cavalier identity and a performed wedding are not established. Evelyn's voiced vignette still waits for the Cavalier; closing invitation is a later cinematic object, not a supplied reunion scene. Red royal letter remains sealed, no contents or mandatory sequel invented. The later fingerprint follow-up verifies the recorded source blobs after CRLF-to-LF conversion only; this does not verify names or exact speech against audio. Retrospective bonus material does not add played events. Paired closing cues: P2344–2345 (~02:46:38–02:46:50) renders Evelyn Blackwood / Lord Ethan Renault travail; Q3909–3911 (~02:46:38–02:46:48) renders Evelyn Blackwood / Lord Ethan Renald Travail, then GCT. Agreement on GCT is text evidence, not an original written signature or an expanded identity. P2346–2347 / Q3912–3914 describes a red letter from the kingdom that may be opened someday, followed by the campaign-ending line near 02:46:59. No red seal design is specified. [Selected paired-cue follow-up](../../reports/DELVERS_INVITATION_CUES_2026-10-28.md).
